%I A070226 #18 Aug 07 2026 00:57:14
%S A070226 1,2,9,12,18,80,448,625,720,1200,1250,1620,2025,4032,4050,5625,7500,
%T A070226 9408,11250,11264,20412,35721,53248,71442,101376,117649,235298,280000,
%U A070226 371712,479232,768320,907200,1058841,1114112,1270080,1411788,1750000
%N A070226 Numbers k such that the squarefree kernel of k is equal to the number of divisors of k.
%C A070226 From _Ctibor O. Zizka_, Feb 05 2009: (Start)
%C A070226 Numbers k such that A007947(k)/A000005(k) = 1.
%C A070226 Conjecture : If A000005(k) divides A007947(k) for some k, then A007947(k) = A000005(k). (End)
%C A070226 From _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 06 2026: (Start)
%C A070226 The conjecture above is true: For any k >= 1, A001222(A000005(k)) >= A001221(k) (from the multiplicative formula for A000005). If A000005(k) divides A007947(k), then A000005(k) is squarefree, so A001221(A000005(k)) = A001222(A000005(k)) >= A001221(k). However, because A000005(k) divides A007947(k), we also have A001221(A000005(k)) <= A001221(A007947(k)) = A001221(k). Therefore, A001221(A000005(k)) = A001221(A007947(k)), and A000005(k) cannot be a proper divisor of A007947(k).
%C A070226 The sequence includes 2*p^(p-1) for all odd primes p. (End)
%H A070226 Donovan Johnson, <a href="/A070226/b070226.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..150</a>
%t A070226 Select[Range[2000000],DivisorSigma[0,#]==Times@@Transpose[ FactorInteger[#]][[1]]&] (* _Harvey P. Dale_, Apr 09 2012 *)
%o A070226 (PARI) isok(k) = {my(f = factor(k)); vecprod(f[,1]) == numdiv(f);} \\ _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 06 2026
%Y A070226 Cf. A000005, A001221, A001222, A007947, A033950.
%K A070226 nonn,changed
%O A070226 1,2
%A A070226 _Jason Earls_, May 07 2002
